<article class="env-example" id="05EF">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05EF">Example <span data-tag="05EF">4.7.1.8</span> <span class="named">(Truncated Kan Complexes)</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex. For every integer $n$, the following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="054P" href="/tag/054P">3.5.7.1</a>. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every Kan complex $K$, the Kan complex $\operatorname{Fun}(K,X)$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every simplicial set $K$, the Kan complex $\operatorname{Fun}(K, X)$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated when regarded as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> The implications $(3) \Rightarrow (2) \Rightarrow (1)$ are immediate, the implication $(1) \Rightarrow (3)$ follows from Corollary <a data-tag="056T" href="/tag/056T">3.5.9.31</a>, and the equivalence $(2) \Leftrightarrow (4)$ is a special case of Example <a data-tag="06QV" href="/tag/06QV">4.7.1.7</a>. </p>
</article>
